              <td class="p-3">Assess pain, agitation, or titrate antihypertensives.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>MAP vs. Systolic Blood Pressure: Which Number Matters More?</h2>
      <p>
        Systolic pressure reveals peak ejection force, but fluctuates with vascular resistance and anxiety. In contrast, our <strong>bp map calculator</strong> measures continuous driving perfusion. In septic shock, vasoconstriction may falsely preserve systolic numbers while tissues remain hypoxic. Tracking MAP provides a true assessment of microvascular delivery.
      </p>

      <h2>Pulse Pressure (PP) and Arterial Health</h2>
      <p>
        Evaluating Pulse Pressure (PP = SBP - DBP) alongside MAP yields valuable insights:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Normal (30–50 mmHg):</strong> Healthy aortic elasticity and stroke volume.</li>
        <li><strong>Narrow (&lt; 30 mmHg):</strong> Seen in hypovolemic shock, cardiac tamponade, or severe aortic stenosis.</li>
        <li><strong>Wide (&gt; 60 mmHg):</strong> Common in arterial stiffness, aortic regurgitation, or severe hypertension.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions (Clinical &amp; Nursing FAQ)</h2>
      
      <div class="space-y-4 my-6">
        <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
