              3. How is MAP calculated from blood pressure?
              <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
            </h3>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
            Using our <strong>map calculator bp</strong>, the formula is MAP = DBP + ⅓(SBP - DBP), or [(2 × DBP) + SBP] ÷ 3. Diastolic pressure is doubled because the cardiac cycle spends twice as long in diastole.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="group rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-4.5 dark:border-slate-800 dark:bg-slate-900">
          <summary class="cursor-pointer list-none">
            <h3 class="flex items-center justify-between font-bold text-slate-900 dark:text-white">
              4. Why is 65 mmHg the target MAP in septic shock?
              <span class="group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform">▼</span>
            </h3>
          </summary>
          <p class="mt-2 text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-300">
            Surviving Sepsis guidelines target 65 mmHg because clinical evidence proves it maintains adequate tissue perfusion without the arrhythmias and excessive vasoconstriction caused by higher vasopressor doses.
